Fold-to-fold standard deviation of PR-AUC is {{cv_pr_auc_sd}}, larger than most differences between pipelines; that is why pipelines are compared pairwise on the same folds (corrected resampled t-test) rather than by eyeballing their means.

## Decision threshold and costs

The threshold {{threshold}} minimises expected cost on dev out-of-fold predictions (best threshold in each of the 3 CV repeats: {{thr_repeat_range}}). It was fixed before the holdout was scored.

**{{cost_label}}.** Accepted for this project as round, illustrative values:

| Outcome | Assumed cost |
|---|---|
| Any step-up (review / OTP) | {{cost_review}} USD |
| Good customer stepped up (extra friction) | +{{cost_friction}} USD |
| Missed fraud | {{cost_fn_frac}} × amount + {{cost_fn_fixed}} USD |

Each value was scaled ×0.5 and ×2: the cost-optimal threshold moved between {{sens_thr_min}} and {{sens_thr_max}}; the largest extra cost from keeping the base threshold was {{sens_worst_regret}} per 1,000 transactions ({{sens_worst_param}}). **Re-derive the threshold with your own costs** rather than reusing this one.

A supplementary three-tier policy (approve / step-up ≥ {{tt_stepup}} / decline ≥ {{tt_decline}}; assumes {{tt_pass_rate_pct}} of fraud passes step-up and {{tt_decline_fp}} USD per wrongly declined customer) was cheaper than two-tier on CV ({{tt_cv_diff}} per 1,000) but **not distinguishable on the holdout** ({{ho_tt_diff}} per 1,000, 95% CI {{ho_tt_diff_ci}}).

## Explainability

Exact linear SHAP values (coefficient × centred input). The top three features, {{shap_top3}}, carry {{shap_top3_share}} of mean |SHAP|. Example: the largest missed fraud in CV ({{shap_missed_amount}}) had one strong risk signal but lacked the other red flags. SHAP explains the model's log-odds, not causes of fraud.

